  "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n\nLet $Y=\\frac{X}{2}+2.$ Calculate $P(1\\le Y\\le 3).$",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=\\frac{X}{2}+2$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nNow, ${f}_{X}(x)={f}_{Y}(y)$ for corresponding values of $X$ and $Y.$ Therefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ | $\\frac{5}{12}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{12}$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le Y\\le 3) & =\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{5}{12}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
